= Installing Oracle 8.1.6 or 8.1.7 on Redhat 7.x =

8.1.6 and 8.1.7 require a particilar JRE be installed. Download '''jre118_v3-glibc-2.1.3.tar.bz2''' (not a newer version). This jre is hard to come by these days but can currently be downloaded from [http://ftp.vim.org/languages/java/jdk/JDK-1.1.8/i386/v3/ vim]. Download it and execute the following commands as root.

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
mv jre118_v3-glibc-2.1.3.tar.bz2 /usr/local
cd /usr/local
bunzip2 jre118_v3-glibc-2.1.3.tar.bz2
tar xf jre118_v3-glibc-2.1.3.tar
ln -s jre118_v3 java
rm jre118_v3-glibc-2.1.3.tar
}}}
}}}

Add the following line to /home/oracle/.bashrc near the bottom:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
export LD_ASSUME_KERNEL=2.2.5
}}}
}}}

Download the following RPM's from [http://rpmfind.net rpmfind] or apt-get them if your system is set up to use apt.

* '''compat-glibc-6.2-2.1.3.2.i386.rpm''' for !RedHat Linux 7.2
* '''compat-egcs-6.2-1.1.2.16.i386.rpm''' for !RedHat Linux 7.2

You can install them using //rpm -i//.

Now edit /home/oracle/.bashrc and add the following command near the bottom.

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
. /usr/i386-glibc21-linux/bin/i386-glibc21-linux-env.sh
}}}
}}}

Now, log in as the oracle user.

Since Oracle 8.1.6 and 8.1.7 use a graphical installer, you need to be running X Windows or point the DISPLAY environment variable to an X Windows session running somewhere.

I have had problems with the graphical installer on enlightenment and fluxbox window managers in the past, though they may work for you.

Installing Oracle 8.1.6 and 8.1.7 tend to be easy and painless. If you have a CD, mount it and cd to the mount point. If you have a tar.gz distribution, extract it using //tar xfz// and cd to the Disk1 directory created by the extraction.

Type '''./runInstaller'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Welcome screen''' click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''File Locations screen'''...[[br]]Check the '''Destination''', it should be the same as the ORACLE_HOME Environment variable.[[br]]Click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Available Products''' screen...[[br]]Select '''Oracle8i Enterprise Edition 8.1.7'''[[br]]Click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Installation Types''' screen[[br]]Select '''Typical'''[[br]]Click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Database Identification''' screen...[[br]]Enter ora1 for '''Global Database Name''' and '''SID'''[[br]]Click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Database File Location''' screen...[[br]]For '''Directory for Database Files''' enter /u01[[br]]Click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Choose JDK Home Directory''' screen...[[br]]For '''Enter JDK Home''' enter /usr/local/java[[br]]Click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Summary''' screen click '''Install'''[[br]][[br]] The '''Install''' screen will show the progress of the installation

If you get an error such as "Error invoking target install of makefile /u01/app/oracle/product8/8.1.7/ctx/lib/ins_ctx.mk", switch to another screen and edit the file /u01/app/oracle/product8/8.1.7/ctx/lib/env_ctx.mk. Search for a line starting with INSO_LINK and append $(LDLIBFLAG)dl to the end of the line. Then click Retry.

At the end of the installation, a Dialog will pop up asking you to run root.sh. Just follow the directions on the dialog and click '''Ok'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Configuration Tools''' screen, the Net8 Configuration Dialog will pop up[[br]]Check '''Perform typical configuration'''[[br]]Click '''Next'''[[br]][[br]] A dialog will pop up indicating that a database is being created[[br]]After a while, a dialog will pop up indicating that database creation completed[[br]]Click '''Ok''' On the '''End Of Installation''' screen click '''Exit'''[[br]][[br]] On the '''Do you really want to exit?''' dialog click '''Yes'''

